 ## Refunding an order
 
-Refunds are issued by support tooling, not by the API. Support calls
-`RefundLedger.record_refund` through the internal console, which refunds the
-charge in full and marks the order `refunded`.
+```
+POST /orders/{order_id}/refunds
+{ "amount_cents": 2500, "reason": "damaged in transit" }
+```
+
+Requires the `refunds:write` scope. Omit `amount_cents` to refund whatever is
+still refundable, which is what the support console does.
+
+An order may be refunded more than once. The sum of refunds may not exceed
+`total_cents`, and an order may not be refunded more than
+`orders.max_refunds_per_order` times. Either violation returns `409`.
 
-An order can be refunded once. A second attempt raises `RefundError`.
+`OrderResponse` reports progress through `refunded_cents`; the `fully_refunded`
+boolean is gone. An order is fully refunded when
+`refunded_cents == total_cents`, and its status is `partially_refunded` in
+between.
diff --git a/migrations/0044_partial_refunds.sql b/migrations/0044_partial_refunds.sql
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a59ac2b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/migrations/0044_partial_refunds.sql
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+-- Partial refunds: track a running refunded total per order.
+--
+-- Backfill runs inline. orders is ~4.1M rows on production; the UPDATE takes
+-- roughly two minutes and holds a row lock per batch, not a table lock.
+
+BEGIN;
+
+ALTER TABLE orders
+    ADD COLUMN refunded_cents integer NOT NULL DEFAULT 0;
+
+UPDATE orders o
+   SET refunded_cents = coalesce(r.total, 0)
+  FROM (
+        SELECT order_id, sum(amount_cents) AS total
+          FROM refunds
+      GROUP BY order_id
+       ) r
+ WHERE r.order_id = o.id;
+
+UPDATE orders
+   SET status = 'partially_refunded'
+ WHERE refunded_cents > 0
+   AND refunded_cents < total_cents;
+
+ALTER TABLE orders
+    DROP COLUMN fully_refunded;
+
+ALTER TABLE orders
+    ADD CONSTRAINT orders_refunded_within_total
+    CHECK (refunded_cents <= total_cents);
+
+COMMIT;
